<div class="g-prose">
  <p>Reader exports read their script list directly from <code>src/opinion.njk</code>. Shared support, Find, citation, popover and selection composables load before <code>opinion_tabs.js</code>, which composes the existing <code>opinionTabs</code> interface. Keep that canonical order rather than loading the controller alone.</p>
  <p><code>layouts/base.njk</code> loads every page script with <code>defer</code> before <code>alpine.min.js</code>. That order matters: the CSP build starts Alpine as soon as it loads, and a component registered on <code>alpine:init</code> after that never runs.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="g-h2" id="the-csp-rule">The CSP rule</h2>
<div class="g-prose">
  <p>The app ships <code>@alpinejs/csp</code>, the build CourtListener uses, so a page needs no <code>unsafe-eval</code>. Templates may only name a component's members: <code>x-show="rowVisible"</code>, <code>x-on:click="toggleFilter"</code>. Expressions such as <code>x-show="open &amp;&amp; !busy"</code> belong in a getter on the component instead. Every component in <code>assets/js/alpine/components/</code> follows this, and so must a new one.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="g-h2" id="styles">Styles</h2>
<div class="g-prose">
  <p>The CSS entry point is <code>assets/css/styles.css</code>. It imports feature files under <code>assets/css/components/</code> in cascade order, then builds through Tailwind. Component classes such as <code>.severity-badge</code> and <code>.evidence-card</code> use <code>@apply</code> against <code>tailwind.config.js</code>; one-off styling is inline utilities. <code>adaptive-overrides.css</code> and <code>layout-overrides.css</code> intentionally stay last for cross-feature responsive, panel and toolbar rules. A part's classes come with the stylesheet, so in the app there is nothing to add. In another Tailwind 3 project, copy the theme, fonts, CSS entry point and its imported files; the Files section of each page names them.</p>
</div>

